"""Original myopathy chapter: inflammatory patterns, diagnosis and treatment."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
BS=ref('BSR Guideline for Idiopathic Inflammatory Myopathy','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9398208/')
CA=ref('IMACS Myositis-Associated Cancer Screening Guideline','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10834225/')
IB=ref('Inclusion Body Myositis Diagnostic Criteria','https://doi.org/10.1111/joim.12480')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))

q('Progressive difficulty rising from a chair and lifting the arms occurs with normal sensation. Which localization is favored?', 'A proximal myopathic process',[
 ('A purely distal sensory neuropathy','That does not explain proximal motor dysfunction without sensory loss.'),('An isolated optic neuropathy','It cannot explain the limb weakness.'),('A single common fibular nerve lesion','The distribution involves multiple proximal muscle groups.')],
 'Distribution guides the first localization', '**Many myopathies preferentially affect shoulder and hip girdles.**', ['Neck flexor and bulbar weakness may coexist.','Reflexes can remain preserved until weakness is advanced.'], '**Confirm the pattern and consider mimics.**', ['Fatigable junctional weakness and endocrine disease can resemble myopathy.','Use CK, physiology and other testing according to the phenotype.'], [['Proximal pattern','Suggests muscle'],['Sensation','Usually preserved'],['Confirmation','Clinical and laboratory integration']], [BS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which statement about CK in suspected inflammatory myopathy is correct?', 'A normal or modest CK does not exclude every inflammatory myopathy phenotype',[
 ('All active myositis requires CK above 10,000','CK varies substantially by subtype and activity.'),('Any CK elevation proves autoimmune myositis','Exercise, drugs and other muscle disorders can elevate CK.'),('CK alone measures swallowing and respiratory safety','These require direct clinical assessment.')],
 'A muscle enzyme is an imperfect activity marker', '**CK reflects muscle membrane injury rather than every aspect of function.**', ['Amyopathic skin disease and selected myositis phenotypes may have low muscle enzymes.','Chronic damage can coexist with little ongoing enzyme release.'], '**Assess more than a number.**', ['Combine strength, extramuscular findings and imaging or biopsy where indicated.','Investigate nonimmune causes of unexplained elevation.'], [['CK','Injury marker'],['Weakness','Function assessment'],['Normal CK','Not universal exclusion']], [BS])
q('Proximal weakness is accompanied by violaceous eyelid discoloration and scaly papules over the knuckles. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Dermatomyositis',[
 ('Inclusion body myositis','The characteristic skin pattern is not its defining presentation.'),('Pure steroid myopathy','This does not account for the characteristic rash.'),('Lambert–Eaton syndrome','Autonomic symptoms and facilitation are more typical than this cutaneous syndrome.')],
 'Skin findings identify a myositis phenotype', '**Heliotrope change and Gottron papules are characteristic DM features.**', ['Skin disease can occur with little clinically evident muscle weakness.','Assess lung, swallowing and other systemic involvement.'], '**Do not restrict evaluation to CK.**', ['Use myositis-specific antibodies and tissue assessment appropriately.','Adult-onset disease warrants risk-informed cancer screening.'], [['Heliotrope','Eyelid rash'],['Gottron papules','Extensor-joint papules'],['Systemic review','Lung, swallowing and cancer risk']], [BS,CA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Dermatomyositis is clinically amyopathic but accompanied by new cough, hypoxemia and anti-MDA5 antibodies. What is the major urgent concern?', 'Rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease',[
 ('Normal CK excludes serious disease','Lung disease can be severe despite limited muscle involvement.'),('The rash alone determines respiratory risk','Clinical and antibody context matter.'),('A routine annual review is sufficient for new hypoxemia','The respiratory change requires urgent assessment.')],
 'Muscle severity does not define systemic severity', '**Anti-MDA5 disease has an important association with rapidly progressive ILD.**', ['Prompt pulmonary assessment is required.','Infection is a competing and potentially coexisting diagnosis.'], '**Coordinate early specialist treatment.**', ['Assess oxygenation, HRCT and the pace of deterioration.','Immune treatment intensity depends on severity and risks.'], [['Muscle enzymes','Can be modest'],['Anti-MDA5','ILD risk clue'],['New hypoxemia','Urgent review']], [BS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Myositis, interstitial lung disease, inflammatory arthritis, Raynaud symptoms and mechanic’s hands occur together. Which antibody group is most relevant?', 'Antisynthetase antibodies such as anti-Jo-1',[
 ('AChR antibodies','These are associated with MG.'),('AQP4 antibodies','These are associated with NMOSD.'),('Anti-GQ1b alone','This is associated with selected acute neuropathic syndromes.')],
 'The multisystem combination defines an overlap phenotype', '**Antisynthetase syndrome can involve muscle, lung, joints and skin.**', ['The full feature set need not appear simultaneously.','Non-Jo-1 synthetase antibodies can produce related syndromes.'], '**Screen for organ involvement rather than assuming isolated muscle disease.**', ['Lung disease can dominate prognosis.','Choose treatment with the organ pattern in mind.'], [['Anti-Jo-1','Histidyl-tRNA synthetase'],['Lung','Common major manifestation'],['Phenotype','Can evolve over time']], [BS])
q('An adult with new dermatomyositis has anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies and dysphagia. Which additional evaluation is especially important?', 'Risk-stratified malignancy screening beyond routine age-appropriate screening when indicated',[
 ('No cancer assessment if CK improves','Treatment response does not eliminate malignancy risk.'),('A positive antibody proves a specific cancer site','It is a risk marker rather than a tumor-localization test.'),('Every patient needs the same unchanging test package indefinitely','IMACS uses risk and timing to guide screening.')],
 'Subtype and antibody findings modify cancer risk', '**Anti-TIF1-gamma, adult DM and selected clinical features increase concern.**', ['Age at onset, dysphagia and persistent activity contribute to risk assessment.','Anti-NXP2 is another high-risk factor in the IMACS framework.'], '**Use the complete screening guideline.**', ['Maintain routine population screening as well.','A negative first assessment does not end all follow-up in high-risk disease.'], [['Antibody','Risk information'],['Clinical features','Modify risk'],['Screening','Risk- and time-informed']], [CA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Marked proximal weakness and very high CK persist after statin withdrawal; anti-HMGCR antibodies are detected. Which diagnosis is favored?', 'Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy',[
 ('Self-limited statin myalgia as the complete explanation','Persistent objective weakness and antibody evidence favor an immune process.'),('Steroid myopathy before any steroid exposure','The exposure and phenotype do not support this attribution.'),('A pure sensory polyneuropathy','It does not account for the marked muscle injury.')],
 'Persistence after drug withdrawal is a key distinction', '**Anti-HMGCR myopathy can continue after the statin is stopped.**', ['Statin exposure is not mandatory for the disease.','Biopsy often shows prominent necrosis with relatively limited inflammatory infiltrate.'], '**Assess immune treatment through a specialist service.**', ['Severe weakness and dysphagia may need early aggressive care.','Do not repeatedly rechallenge the suspected trigger without expert review.'], [['Toxic statin symptoms','Often improve after withdrawal'],['Anti-HMGCR disease','Can persist'],['Treatment','Immune-directed strategy']], [BS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which myositis-specific antibody is associated with an important immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy subtype?', 'Anti-SRP',[
 ('Anti-GQ1b','This is associated with selected GBS-spectrum syndromes.'),('Anti-AQP4','This defines a different CNS astrocytic disorder.'),('Anti-AChR','This is associated with postsynaptic MG.')],
 'Antibody identity helps classify muscle disease', '**Anti-SRP myopathy can cause severe proximal weakness and substantial CK elevation.**', ['Dysphagia and other severe manifestations can occur.','Clinical and biopsy context remain important.'], '**Plan treatment according to severity.**', ['Some cases require combination immune treatment.','Track strength and function as well as CK.'], [['Anti-SRP','Necrotizing myopathy'],['Anti-HMGCR','Another necrotizing subtype'],['Monitoring','Strength, function and injury markers']], [BS])
q('A 68-year-old has slowly progressive asymmetric finger-flexor and quadriceps weakness with falls and dysphagia. Which diagnosis is most characteristic?', 'Inclusion body myositis',[
 ('Typical symmetric acute polymyositis as the best fit','The selective chronic asymmetric pattern strongly favors IBM.'),('Pure MG','Selective atrophy and chronic finger-flexor weakness are less typical than fatigable transmission failure.'),('An isolated lumbar radiculopathy','This does not explain the characteristic upper- and lower-limb combination.')],
 'IBM breaks the simple proximal-myopathy rule', '**Finger-flexor and knee-extensor weakness is a high-value pattern.**', ['Disease often begins after midlife.','Dysphagia can be clinically important.'], '**Use pattern-aware biopsy and physiology.**', ['Rimmed vacuoles and inflammatory changes can support diagnosis.','Early biopsy may not show every hallmark.'], [['Upper limb','Finger flexors'],['Lower limb','Quadriceps'],['Course','Slow, often asymmetric']], [IB], 'clinical_scenario')
q('How does established IBM generally differ therapeutically from many other inflammatory myopathies?', 'It is usually poorly responsive to conventional immunosuppression, making supportive functional and swallowing care central',[
 ('High-dose steroids reliably restore all lost muscle strength','This overstates the expected benefit.'),('No active care is useful','Rehabilitation, assistive devices and dysphagia care are important.'),('It should always be treated as MG with pyridostigmine alone','The underlying disease is different.')],
 'Inflammatory histology does not guarantee steroid responsiveness', '**IBM has a different treatment-response profile from many immune myositis subtypes.**', ['Avoid prolonged toxic therapy without objective benefit.','Assess for alternative or coexisting treatable pathology when the course is atypical.'], '**Preserve function and reduce complications.**', ['Address falls, mobility aids and swallowing.','Individualized exercise can support conditioning and function.'], [['Immune response','Often limited'],['Care priority','Function and safety'],['Dysphagia','Needs direct assessment']], [IB,BS])
q('Why is muscle MRI useful in suspected inflammatory myopathy?', 'It can show the distribution of edema and chronic fatty change and help choose an appropriate biopsy site',[
 ('Edema alone proves a unique autoimmune subtype','Edema is not disease-specific.'),('It replaces all clinical and tissue assessment in every patient','Imaging is one component of diagnosis.'),('A severely fatty end-stage muscle is always the best biopsy target','It may provide limited active diagnostic tissue.')],
 'Imaging distinguishes activity patterns from damage patterns', '**Fluid-sensitive signal can suggest active injury, whereas fatty replacement suggests chronic damage.**', ['Exercise, denervation and other disorders can also produce signal changes.','Sampling a moderately affected suitable muscle may improve biopsy yield.'], '**Interpret the image with the phenotype.**', ['Antibodies, CK, examination and pathology answer complementary questions.','Avoid diagnosing solely from edema.'], [['Edema','Possible active injury'],['Fat','Chronic damage'],['Biopsy planning','Select informative tissue']], [BS])
q('Which treatment principle applies to significant active non-IBM inflammatory myopathy?', 'Use glucocorticoids with appropriate steroid-sparing therapy, tailored to severity and organ involvement',[
 ('All patients require identical lifelong high-dose prednisone','Toxicity and disease phenotype require individualization.'),('Exercise must be permanently prohibited','Appropriate supervised exercise is part of care.'),('Treat CK without assessing weakness, lung or swallowing function','Those manifestations can determine urgency and treatment choice.')],
 'Treat active disease while limiting cumulative harm', '**Early steroid-sparing treatment can reduce prolonged glucocorticoid exposure.**', ['Methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate and other options fit different contexts.','Severe organ disease may require more intensive strategies.'], '**Monitor response across domains.**', ['Assess strength, function, skin, lung and swallowing as relevant.','Plan infection and bone-health prevention.'], [['Initial control','Glucocorticoid'],['Longer-term strategy','Steroid-sparing agent'],['Targets','Muscle and extramuscular disease']], [BS])
q('A patient with active myositis has repeated choking and weight loss. What is the most appropriate response?', 'Urgent swallowing and nutritional assessment with disease treatment and aspiration-risk management',[
 ('Wait for CK normalization before assessing swallowing','Aspiration and malnutrition are immediate risks.'),('Assume all dysphagia is functional','Inflammatory muscle disease can affect swallowing.'),('Continue unrestricted intake regardless of aspiration','The feeding plan must follow safety assessment.')],
 'Bulbar disease is a treatment and safety priority', '**Dysphagia can cause aspiration, dehydration and malnutrition.**', ['Speech/swallow and nutrition teams may be needed.','The cause can include active inflammation or chronic structural/functional damage.'], '**Coordinate a safe feeding and immune-treatment plan.**', ['Severe refractory disease may require additional therapies.','Monitor respiratory complications.'], [['Swallowing','Assess directly'],['Nutrition','Protect intake and weight'],['Treatment','Activity- and severity-guided']], [BS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which role can IVIG have in inflammatory myopathy?', 'A treatment option for selected severe or refractory muscle disease, including some dysphagia scenarios',[
 ('A mandatory substitute for all other care in every mild case','Use depends on severity, response and subtype.'),('An intervention that never requires thrombosis or renal-risk assessment','IVIG safety assessment is necessary.'),('Proof that IBM always responds dramatically','IBM has a different response profile.')],
 'Escalation should match an unmet clinical need', '**IVIG is an established option in selected refractory or severe inflammatory muscle disease.**', ['Assess objective response and the specific manifestation being targeted.','Volume, thrombosis and renal risks influence treatment.'], '**Avoid indefinite continuation without benefit assessment.**', ['Follow strength, function and swallowing where relevant.','Combine with rehabilitation and a longer-term plan.'], [['Indication','Selected severe/refractory disease'],['Safety','Patient-specific risks'],['Response','Objective target measures']], [BS])
q('Weakness worsens during prolonged glucocorticoid treatment while CK is normal and other inflammatory features have improved. Which possibility deserves assessment?', 'Glucocorticoid-induced myopathy rather than automatic escalation for an inflammatory flare',[
 ('Any new weakness proves more inflammation','Drug-related weakness and damage are alternatives.'),('Normal CK proves normal muscle function','CK can be normal in steroid myopathy.'),('Abruptly stop all glucocorticoids without a plan','Adrenal suppression and underlying disease need consideration.')],
 'Treatment can create a mimic of disease activity', '**Steroid myopathy commonly causes proximal weakness with little enzyme elevation.**', ['The clinical course and other activity measures help distinguish it.','Deconditioning and chronic muscle damage can contribute.'], '**Adjust therapy safely.**', ['Use a supervised taper and steroid-sparing strategy when appropriate.','Rehabilitation and nutrition support recovery.'], [['Active inflammation','May have other activity signs'],['Steroid myopathy','Often normal CK'],['Management','Safe dose review and rehabilitation']], [BS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What is the appropriate exercise advice for stable treated inflammatory myopathy?', 'Individualized supervised aerobic and resistance exercise according to disease activity and functional capacity',[
 ('Permanent complete rest for every patient','This promotes deconditioning.'),('Unrestricted maximal exercise during severe acute muscle injury','Intensity must fit safety and disease status.'),('Exercise can replace indicated immunotherapy in every case','It complements disease-directed treatment.')],
 'Rehabilitation is part of active treatment', '**Appropriate exercise helps preserve strength, conditioning and participation.**', ['Adjust to fatigue, cardiopulmonary disease and current activity.','Physiotherapy can guide progression.'], '**Define functional goals.**', ['Track transfers, walking and daily tasks.','Avoid both prolonged inactivity and unsafe overload.'], [['Exercise','Individualized'],['Acute severe disease','Modify intensity'],['Goal','Function and conditioning']], [BS])
